const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is cachexia?',
    a: 'Cachexia is the severe wasting syndrome that accompanies many serious illnesses — advanced cancer, chronic kidney disease, heart failure, COPD. It is not simple undernutrition: it is ongoing loss of muscle (with or without fat), driven by the illness itself, and it cannot be fully reversed by feeding alone. It worsens quality of life and survival.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does cachexia have to do with the melanocortin system?',
    a: 'The melanocortin appetite circuit is the mechanism cachexia hijacks. Inflammatory signals from the illness push the hypothalamic melanocortin tone up, over-activating MC4R — the same receptor that restrains appetite. The dial that should keep hunger balanced gets jammed toward "stop eating and burn fuel", which is precisely the wrong setting for a wasting body.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is blocking MC4R a treatment for cachexia?',
    a: 'It is the leading melanocortin strategy, still investigational. If over-active MC4R drives the wasting, an MC4R antagonist should ease it — restoring appetite, lowering the raised energy expenditure, and preserving muscle. TCMCB07 and Pfizer’s PF-07258669 are MC4R antagonists developed on exactly this logic. None is yet approved for cachexia.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is this the opposite of obesity treatment?',
    a: 'Precisely opposite, through the same receptor. In genetic obesity the MC4R pathway is under-driven and setmelanotide, an agonist, turns it back up to restrain appetite. In cachexia the pathway is over-driven and the goal is an antagonist to turn it down and restore appetite. One receptor, two diseases, opposite drugs.',
  },
];

