<PageLayout
  title="Cachexia: the appetite switch turned against the body"
  kicker="The condition"
  description="Cachexia is the wasting of serious illness — and it runs through the melanocortin system backwards. Inflammation forces the MC4R appetite circuit into overdrive, jamming the dial on 'stop eating and burn' exactly when the body needs the opposite. It is the mirror image of melanocortin obesity, and the reason MC4R antagonists exist."
  thread="The same rheostat that fails open in obesity, failing shut here — the appetite brake held down by illness until the body consumes itself."
  updated="2026"
  about={[
    { name: 'Cachexia', type: 'MedicalCondition', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cachexia'] },
    { name: 'Melanocortin 4 receptor', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melanocortin_4_receptor'] },
  ]}
>
  <KeyTakeaways
    items={[
      'Cachexia is the illness-driven wasting syndrome of advanced cancer, chronic kidney disease, heart failure and more — loss of muscle that feeding alone cannot reverse, and a major drag on survival.',
      'It runs through the melanocortin appetite circuit in reverse: inflammatory signals from the illness push hypothalamic melanocortin tone up and over-activate MC4R.',
      'That jams the appetite rheostat toward "stop eating and burn fuel" — driving anorexia, raised energy expenditure, and the breakdown of muscle and fat all at once.',
      'It is the exact mirror of melanocortin obesity: there MC4R is under-driven and the fix is an agonist (setmelanotide); here MC4R is over-driven and the aim is an antagonist.',
      'MC4R antagonists — TCMCB07 and Pfizer’s PF-07258669 — were built on this logic. Blocking the over-active receptor should restore appetite and spare muscle; the approach is still investigational.',
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Almost everything else on this site treats the melanocortin system as something to switch
    <em>on</em>. <strong>Cachexia</strong> is the case where the system is already on too hard,
    and the goal is to turn it down. It is the severe wasting that shadows serious illness —
    advanced cancer above all, but also chronic kidney disease, heart failure, and COPD — and it
    is one of the clearest places where the <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R appetite circuit</a>{' '}
    does real harm by doing its ordinary job at the wrong moment.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>

  <h2>Not starvation — something worse</h2>
  <p>
    It is important to separate cachexia from simple undernutrition, because the difference is
    the whole point. A starving but otherwise healthy body defends itself: it lowers its
    metabolic rate and burns fat while sparing muscle for as long as it can. Cachexia does the
    opposite. It is a state of ongoing muscle loss, driven by the illness itself, in which the
    body keeps burning and breaking down tissue even as intake falls — and crucially, it
    <em>cannot</em> be reversed by feeding alone.<Cite n={2} /> That resistance to nutrition is
    what marks it as an active, signalled process rather than a shortage, and it is why it
    carries such weight in prognosis.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>

  <h2>Where the melanocortin system comes in</h2>
  <p>
    The signalling that drives cachexia converges on the hypothalamus, and the melanocortin
    circuit is a central part of the mechanism. Inflammatory cytokines released by the illness
    push the system toward its appetite-suppressing output: the melanocortin tone rises, and{" "}
    <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a> is over-activated.<Cite n={1} /> Think of the appetite{" "}
    <a href="/receptors/mc4r">rheostat</a> again — the dial that normally balances hunger against
    fullness. In cachexia, inflammation leans on that dial and jams it toward "stop eating and
    burn", so three things happen together: appetite falls, resting energy expenditure rises, and
    muscle and fat are broken down. The circuit built to prevent obesity is, here, helping to
    consume the body.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>

