    source="Melanocortin mechanism per Marks, Ling & Cone, Cancer Res 2001 (PMID 11245447); cachexia definition and mechanisms per Fearon 2011 (PMID 21296615) and Baracos 2018 (PMID 29345251). Schematic."
  >
    <CachexiaDial />
  </Figure>

  <h2>One receptor, two diseases, opposite drugs</h2>
  <p>
    This is the cleanest demonstration on the whole site that a receptor is not "good" or "bad" —
    only set too high or too low. In <a href="/pomc-deficiency">genetic obesity</a> the MC4R
    pathway is under-driven and the treatment is an agonist, <a href="/setmelanotide">
    setmelanotide</a>, to turn it back up and restrain a runaway appetite. In cachexia the pathway
    is over-driven and the treatment logic is the reverse: an <strong>antagonist</strong> to turn
    it down and give appetite back. The same molecule that proves the circuit exists by blocking
    it in the lab — <a href="/shu9119">SHU9119</a> — is the template for that idea, and two drugs
    have carried it toward the clinic: <a href="/tcmcb07">TCMCB07</a>, a brain-penetrant MC4R
    antagonist peptide, and Pfizer’s small-molecule <a href="/pf-07258669">PF-07258669</a>.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>

  <h2>The honest limits</h2>
  <p>
    The melanocortin approach to cachexia is promising and unproven. The mechanism is well
    supported, and MC4R antagonists reverse wasting convincingly in animal models — but turning
    that into an approved human therapy has not yet happened, and the development path has been
    uneven (Pfizer shelved PF-07258669 for business reasons despite reaching Phase 1). Cachexia is
    also multifactorial: the melanocortin circuit is a major lever, not the only one, and real
    cachexia care is multidisciplinary. What this page describes is a strong and specific
    rationale, not a finished treatment.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Cachexia belongs on this site as the dark twin of the appetite pages. It shows the
    melanocortin rheostat failing in the opposite direction from obesity, through the very same
    receptor, and it is the entire reason a class of MC4R <em>antagonists</em> exists at all. For
    the receptor at its centre, see <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a>; for the drugs built to
    block it, see <a href="/tcmcb07">TCMCB07</a> and <a href="/pf-07258669">PF-07258669</a>; and
    for the research antagonist that first proved the circuit, see <a href="/shu9119">SHU9119</a>.
  </p>

